WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Super Dodgeball, thrash, bash, and smash the competition.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:27.800
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.700
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.820
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.140
Dodgeball. Quite possibly the most famous made-up sport.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.900
Am I right, guys? What about it as made up, right?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.460
It's not like a legit sport.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.060
This is one of those, you know, the Ocho things all over again, where it's like,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.940
this is something that started in gym class and is mostly a hazing experiment,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:58.140
but then just people like it, love it so much if they're, you know, if they're not the one being hit by the ball.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.480
And then you can just take out your frustrations on things.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.200
This doesn't have any qualifications of being like a real sport.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.300
I don't know. I feel like that's a little closed-minded of you.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.400
I think, like, if it's got some rules and there's some physical activity involved, it's a...

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.060
And a ball. Yeah, and a ball.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.300
It's got a ball. It's a sport, you know?

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.980
Yeah, there you go. All right, and I see what you're saying.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:27.040
It's like, I think, technically, yes, I would say it is definitely a sport.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.580
It has all the makings of a regular old sport.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.400
But yeah, just like, culturally, we don't consider it among, like, baseball and soccer.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.980
So I'd say to that point, I think it's the most famous of those, like, secondary sports.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.420
Sure. Well, thank you for not taking me so literally.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:44.700
I appreciate that.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.180
But, you know, no, it's just like one of those things, right?

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.960
Everybody knows dodgeball, but you kind of stopped playing it after sixth grade.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:53.760
You know, like, I don't know.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.800
Did you guys ever, like, get really into dodgeball and, like, start a team and do, like, a rec league or anything?

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:10.060
No. I mean, but we did play it through, like, high school, except we had to call it something else because, you know, the kids were getting bullied,

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.700
and you can't play dodgeball.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:18.880
So, but I mean, yeah, I never decided to continue to play it when I didn't need to.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:33.400
Yeah, I think, actually, we have, like, a, I don't know, we would never join, like, a rec league, but I remember times where we would go to just, like, a local place and rent out the gym and play dodgeball with a bunch of people.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.700
Like, kind of random times, but it definitely happened more than once as an adult.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:52.440
Now, was it the, did you have a group of players that surrounded the outskirts of the opposing team, and then three of you were on one side of the ball?

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.860
Or was it all of you were on one side of the ball?

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:05.960
That's exactly what I was going to say, Sean, because, I mean, there's dodgeball, the game where, like, you get hit and you're immediately, you know, eliminated if you get hit if you haven't caught the ball.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:22.960
And then there's this dodgeball, which has, yeah, it has your, you have players on the outside of the arena that you can pass, oh, sorry, the outside of the court inside the arena that you can pass the ball to that can then also attack players on the same side of the court of an opposite team.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:30.000
An interesting, you know, extra element here, but I don't think this is the dodgeball that most of us grew up with.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.580
Certainly not the one where you just continue to pummel people with the ball.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.260
Until they can't take it anymore.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.380
Until their life meter. Yeah, until they're just like, they're like, I'm out.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.680
Like, my health meter is no more.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.900
I can't do this.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.640
I don't even know why I came today.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.520
I'm going home.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.900
Well, actually, I think the little visual representation of this game suggests that they die, right?

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.580
They turn into an angel and fly up to heaven.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.840
They had a good run.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:06.180
Yeah. Which is, which is okay until, you know, when you make it to the next round, your, your angels come back to life.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.720
So, maybe, maybe that's part of the dodgeball gods.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.920
You know, like in Dragon Ball, if you collect the seven Dragon Balls, you get a wish.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.920
Maybe you can use the dodgeball to make a wish after you beat your opponent to bring your other teammates back to life.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.420
Yeah, I think that is in the league charter.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:34.520
All right, so if we're not describing this well enough, just to get into the actual objective of super dodgeball versus what you might be familiar with in dodgeball,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:41.180
the objective of each match is to defeat the rival team by throwing the dodgeball at the other members, the opponents.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.840
Each character has a life gauge that is gradually depleted.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:50.000
It's not, it depends on how many hit points they take by the kind of hit you throw at them.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:59.940
After their life gauge is depleted, they are out, but the other members still remain and you have to remove every one of your opponents.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.100
Is this, yeah.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.560
Is this the first time, it sounds like you're reading something.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.080
This is the first time we've described a game, but it sounds like reading something.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.620
Or did you write this beforehand?

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:18.220
Well, I thought it was important to give, I did write this beforehand, reading right up the show notes, but it's hard to think about.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.060
It is hard to describe.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.400
This is a new rule book for a sport.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.900
You know, it's not like, you would never describe Super Mario Brothers with rules.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.100
Yeah, yeah. But this, because it's not, but this has actual rules to understand.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:39.040
And like, the court is, you know, just like a, it's a free roaming square, which you can only stay on your end of.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.300
There's just like a lot of, I guess, like nuance here.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.120
Yeah. Obviously, like we always link the long plays in the show notes.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.440
So I would, I would watch one of those if it's still not, if you're still not quite getting it.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.960
But I think, I think everybody, we can move on and just talk about the game.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:02.180
It's like, it's like hyper hockey, which has like three pucks and it's actually played on concrete and everybody has a gun with one bullet in it.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:11.660
What? It just like, that's what this version of dodgeball is like to actual dodgeball.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:20.120
I'll say, I feel like the easiest way for me to kind of understand it is it's the way you imagine like professional dodgeball with the line dividing it.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.980
Team on each side. Yeah, like, you know, like dodgeball.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.200
Like the movie. Like the Ocho dodgeball, yeah.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:32.980
Except the only thing I've never seen before is what we were just talking about with like, when you, when somebody is out,

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:38.240
they go to the other side and stand on the sidelines and can catch the ball, pass it and even throw it.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:39.960
Oh yeah. It's like, that's like the one difference.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.620
Yes. There's no bringing people back.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:44.980
Wait, but that's not when they're out, Joe.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.960
They're just, that's just part of your team.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.440
Like you assemble your squad.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.180
You put people you want on the outside.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:52.520
Oh, I thought, okay.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:53.560
I misunderstood.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:07:00.860
Yeah. I thought Joe was talking about how in real dodgeball, you're out, but then you can come back in if you catch it.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.480
If somebody else on the team catches it.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.100
But is that house rules?

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.400
I think that's, I think that's regular rules.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:08.760
Yeah. I'm not sure.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.200
You know, it's kind of like capture the flag.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.820
Everybody has different rules, but ultimately it's just get the flag from the guy's pocket.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.320
Yeah. I think that there are tons of different, I mean, we used to play, not to go on another tangent about like the way we used to play this,

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:28.540
but we used to play in gym class where anyone who was out, if someone on your team, there were dodgeballs and there were also footballs laying around the field.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:37.700
And if anyone on your team threw a football at the backboard to the basketball net and hit it, everybody on the other, everybody who was out from your team got to come back in.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.440
Oh, wow. I mean, clearly just a house rule.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.400
Yeah, that's some golden snitch shit right there.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:51.520
Yeah. It's also very easy to hit the backboard like now, but we used to think, you know, we were like in third grade and it was like, if you hit the backboard, you were like, you were like a superhero.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.300
Like everyone would cheer in and shit.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.160
Anyway, that's, that's not in this game.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.340
So to start this game, there's a bunch of different modes and a bunch of different difficulties.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.340
And this is all decided right at the beginning in the menu screen.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:15.380
And I just thought it was interesting that the menu lets you select both mode and difficulty at the same time.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.480
It's usually a little more like step by step, like separate screens and separate decisions.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.460
This was just like the ultimate decision maker.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.540
I can just free, free wheel my way through the menu.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.900
And I know that sounds silly, but it was just a noticeable difference from anything we've ever seen on the NES yet.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:39.020
So more games should just let you control all the menus on one screen if you should, if you should wish to do so.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:45.740
Because I hate when you, you know, make a few selections, then make one wrong selection and there's no backs.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:49.020
You have to reset the game to make like all the proper decisions again.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.260
This one, you get some time to think about.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.180
Yeah. I didn't really think of that.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:58.040
I just wanted to point out that this is, you know, if Mike is happy with the menu, you know, it's a good menu.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.520
Yeah, it's no Sesame Street, one, two, three.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.140
Then, you know, once you get to, let's say, you know, let's talk about the big mode here, the World Cup mode.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.620
That's the one that probably most people are going to jump to right away.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.980
That one, you select the team based on the country.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.260
And no matter which country you pick, every single member has individual stats.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.200
It's not just for the country itself.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:22.820
It's the team members.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:40.040
And, you know, the objective to give them stats and names is a pretty cool thing, you know, because you put a lot of thought into both the stats of where they should be positionally because you get to, like, take out, like, you know, I don't want, this guy has a lot of throwing power.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:40.980
So I don't want him on the outside.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.080
I want him on the inside.

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:45.240
Or, you know, this guy has a lot of health.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.460
So he should be, you know, part of my team that's taking the damage regularly.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.720
You could think of it like that.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:10:00.540
Or you could just, like, try out each team and stick with the defaults because they probably know better than most casual players on how to do this thing.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.220
I'm curious, Sean and Joe, what did you guys do for the teams?

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.140
Did you actually think about this strategically with the stats and everything?

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:08.920
Or did you just take the default team?

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:14.000
Well, I was under the impression that during regular World Cup mode, you could only play as the United States.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.120
Yeah, I think that's true.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.800
But I'm just talking about in general, like, when you select.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:20.520
Oh, like your player lineup?

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.000
Yeah, yeah, your player lineup and stuff.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.060
I went with the default.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:37.420
I feel like if they designed this game regularly, they would have at least made it, like, a moderately intuitive default setup.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:51.780
I know that, like, I think the Sam, or I forgot his actual name, like, he's got some pretty useful moves and he's sort of like the captain or however you want to describe it.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:58.680
And I figure, like, yeah, that's the kind of player that I would want to keep where he is, where the game put him.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.840
So I didn't really play around too much with the lineups.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:30.820
I didn't.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:38.080
The first time I was facing India, I think, which was like match three or four, my starting lineup was, like, just not doing any damage.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:44.940
Then for the next match, I switched my starting lineup and made them, you know, put them on the sidelines and everyone was doing much better again.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:46.700
And then a couple matches later, I had to switch again.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.400
It could just be my imagination.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.780
Maybe I was just getting tired and then the placebo effect of me switching made me better again.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.880
But I really felt like there was something to switching it.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.740
But, you know, I can't confirm that there actually is anything in the code that I want to say.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:08.680
I want to say that that's that's more like a reflection of the stats of the teams you're playing.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:14.740
Like maybe that they have like there's a lot of variables that I don't even know what a lot of them do.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:25.800
But it's I guess it's possible that like catch frequency would make you feel like you're not throwing the ball hard enough because they're catching it more or something along that those lines.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.440
Yeah, honestly, that that might might be it for sure, too.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:35.940
And it might have just worked out that the next team worked better with my my new lineup.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:44.060
Yeah. Yeah, I can't find anything that suggests that they get tired or that you would need to change them.

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:53.360
I was thinking until you mentioned that you were swapping out the players and noticing a difference that maybe you were just getting tired as it went along.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:13:05.260
But, you know, knowing that it changes a little bit, I would say that there is a noticeable progression, you know, as you move on through the World Cup at any difficulty.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:08.020
Right. Of the stats of the players you're going against.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:18.540
So, you know, the first level is designed kind of to be a cakewalk where they don't really throw the ball really all that smart, but also just like don't do much damage.

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:23.440
But then the later guys have beefed up stats that are going to, you know, hurt you if you're not playing smart.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:36.480
In terms of like the statistics Sean was referencing and like is it is strange that like things such as their haircuts can matter to like to the kind of playing style they have.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:45.280
Yeah, I didn't take the time to learn all of that and I trusted the game to just give me the default lineup that was like this is this is what's best for you.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:48.200
But it's always nice to go with the extra right.

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:55.100
We always appreciate that to like have the extras even just naming them is a little more flavor than we're used to on the NES.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:06.780
So giving them names and having, you know, stats and being able to change the position of those players, not just saying like, oh, well, if you play as the US, they have really strong arms.

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.520
Whereas if you play with China, they have really good defense.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:10.480
You know, I'm making this up.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.820
I'm not saying these are like representative of those nations.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:21.480
But the idea that like it's actually on an individual player basis, I feel like that can only improve the longevity of the game.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:29.360
Oh, yeah. Yeah. And and not even just that, but also like the the fact that a lot of these courts have different.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:36.000
I guess I'm jumping ahead a bit, but like just in terms of like replayability and even some of the courts have different like effects.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:49.780
Like if you're playing against Iceland, the courts a little bit more slippery or if you're playing against Kenya, the courts a little bit softer and you have to pick up more speed if you're trying to run for longer.

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:56.700
There's a lot of different things going on here that make each game feel a little bit different.

00:14:56.980 --> 00:15:01.420
Yeah. It's a little bit like tennis in that regard, you know, with the the clay courts versus the grass courts.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:18.220
Yeah. So, you know, yeah, the attention to detail both in the individual stats of the players, the the courts and even just like the main appeal of the of the gameplay for me was just the variety in your movement and throws.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:28.520
You can you can you can just throw the ball, you can pass it around, you can throw in the air, you can do a running throw and you can do a running jump throw.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:32.140
And some of these will even lead to what is known as power throws.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:37.360
So it's not even though it's just one button that throws the ball and one button that passes the ball.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:44.020
The movement intertwined with that creates a lot of like unique circumstances to happen.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:50.120
And the computer probably takes advantage of it better than I can because they understand the nuances of it.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:16:06.260
But it does feel good to be in control of what you want to do with with so little buttons to press, whereas they could have made this a very complicated thing where you have to build up like a stat meter to then get a power throw.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:10.500
And then you your next throw is automatically that power throw, like whether you like it or not.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:21.560
So I did appreciate that they decided to make movement in a game like dodgeball where, you know, the whole idea is to run away from the ball or run to pick up the ball.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:23.720
Movement is such a large part of this game.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:24.900
I wasn't expecting that.

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.660
I was expecting to be more just about targeting.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:35.060
Yeah, that's one of the like the main descriptors I would I would give this game is that it is satisfying to play.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:55.960
And it's like it's it's not that, you know, right off the bat, like how like how to pull off these super moves like in the super moves are like where the ball will either go super fast or it'll split in three or it'll take some weird trajectory and blink and be all energy looking.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:02.620
But like over time, just as you're playing, you'll sort of get a rhythm for it and be like, oh, how do I do that?

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:04.760
And then you'll try to do it again and maybe you'll fail.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:07.120
But then over time, you'll get that rhythm.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:12.740
And it just feels really good to like once you master it and now you're just wrecking shit.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:24.920
Yeah, I think my like one of my favorite things about this game is the fact that it's it's so it feels so easy to learn, like compared to like some other other sports games or especially like those Olympic style games.

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:28.100
Like this is easy to pick up and it's it's simple in that sense.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:35.100
Like but it's still it's still complex, like you can still do a lot once you learn the controls, which you pick up really quickly.

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:36.280
It feels intuitive.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.740
And like I'd say it's complex, but not complicated.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.540
You know, it's not like you have to do all these weird button schemes.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:49.060
It's just you get the feel of it really fast and you can do a lot with that with that intuitive control scheme.

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:56.460
Yeah, that's like that's a good thought, Joe, because there's no like there's no penalties that you can possibly like incur.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:02.940
You know, you're stuck to the confines of the buttons you have and you can't move across the courts or anything.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:15.720
But this game is completely playable in a very boring scenario where like when you're first learning it, you just have the possession of the ball and you throw it and there's no penalty for them catching it or anything like that.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:16.540
So it's just OK.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:17.840
It didn't do any damage.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:22.780
It's perfectly serviceable to like learn how to play this game just through experiment.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:24.720
No need to actually have the manual.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:35.840
And then you as you keep playing, you start to realize like, oh, when I, you know, press twice on the D pad, like now I'm running and my running throws seem to have a little more power to them.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:38.520
And now I'm actually seeing health points be taken away.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:49.780
I think it's interesting that this game can be played, you know, maybe not properly, but like can be played by anybody who barely even understands it just by pressing A and B.

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:53.940
Right. Like you'll you'll just continuously pass it and then eventually lob it at somebody.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:19:01.700
But then it also like rewards those who take the time to learn the system and can probably deal with the opponents much quicker.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:11.540
Yeah, it's like that classic description of sports, like I guess even like e-sports games where it's like got a it's got a low skill floor and a high skill ceiling.

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:12.900
I mean, not not super high.

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:22.600
There's not too much crazy shit you can do in this game, but that there is you'll start to learn about like intercepting the ball when they're trying to jump.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:34.760
When I'm going to try to throw it behind you and or just blocking it and taking that one that one damage to make sure that something crazier doesn't happen.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:39.060
So, yeah, that's that's pretty much it for me.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:46.400
Yeah. And there's just not like a there's not like I could control scheme to like have to memorize a bunch of things, too.

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:52.820
It's like, oh, I want to I want to learn how to pull off this this move where, you know, it's not like you have to hold this button and this button and that.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.240
But it's just it's just intuitive.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:55.640
It's just you just do that.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.260
Like, you know how to move in that direction.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:58.240
You know how to throw faster.

00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:02.480
You know how to jump. Do whatever combination of those things you want to do whatever move you want.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.520
And yeah, like you said, Sean, it's like a ton of things you can do, but everything you can do.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.900
Once you get those controls down, you could just figure out intuitively how to do it.

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:39.040
And this is going to piss off some fighting game fans, but the power throws could have been something like, you know, ultimate moves in in Street Fighter or something where, like, you know,

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:46.240
you have to input a certain amount of, you know, fatalities in Mortal Kombat where you have to, like, hit the certain amount of D-pad things in the right order.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:51.020
And then you'll execute these power throws that otherwise, like, noobs wouldn't know about.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:53.300
But instead, it just has to do with, like, timing.

00:20:53.580 --> 00:21:01.100
And even if you don't understand that or don't use the manual, there's a good chance, like, in any particular round of gameplay,

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:06.280
you're just going to accidentally throw one just because of how, like, even though it feels a little random,

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:08.520
it does have to do with some kind of timing.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:10.340
It doesn't guarantee a hit.

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:16.240
Like, you can, like, waste a power throw if you're not, like, intentionally throwing it at the right person.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:20.560
And I'm really surprised by how many different power throws there were.

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:25.800
I think there was, like, eight ground power throws and eight different aerial power throws,

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.280
which was just, you know, fun to see.

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:29.720
I don't even think I saw them all, to be honest.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:33.460
But the ones I did see, it's, like, surprisingly cartoonish.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:34.600
Oh, yeah. Like, how they work.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:42.420
Yeah. I mean, my favorite was just the standard, like, throw it really fast and it doesn't stop when it hits somebody.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:49.960
So, you can, if the computer's dumb enough to, like, just stack everyone, you basically knock everyone down with double-digit HP.

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:56.620
Yeah. So, I would try my best to do that over and over because then the guy behind him would catch it.

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:01.480
So, like, yeah. You can also, like, develop your own strategies of, like, the fastest way to dispatch these guys.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:08.700
I imagine on the higher difficulty, I didn't play above normal, but I imagine on the higher difficulty, it's a little bit more difficult to do that.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:16.860
But, yeah, there's a lot of playground to play around with different strategies.

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:22.080
And on the cartoonish note, it is a little surprising, and I'm not saying it would be for the best here.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:32.140
I actually think their show of restraint actually helped them a little bit because, you know, the characters themselves are cartoonish and the designs of the stages are cartoonish.

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:48.480
You would think that there would be, like, maybe some pitfalls or obstacles that might frequently appear to make the dodgeball match a little more, like, silly or something like that, given the way that the ball works and just, like, the characters in general.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:51.460
But they chose not to do something like that.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.920
And I think that's for the best, to be honest.

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:55.040
But I just wanted to throw it out there.

00:22:55.140 --> 00:23:00.080
Do you guys think that some kind of stage hazards could have helped this game at all?

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:03.500
I don't know. I think it would make it a different game.

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:10.300
I think it could be fun, but, you know, it's not what they set out to do, and I don't think it needs it by any means.

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.720
Especially if you want to play this with, like, a two-player, right?

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:18.020
Then I feel like you want just, like, the pure competition of it.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:24.040
Yeah, I don't know about hazards, like Joe said, but, like I was saying with Iceland and Kenya,

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:32.940
if there were different variations that didn't give one team an unfair advantage or maybe just, like, different –

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:39.060
you could do it with the shape of the court or you could also do it with, like, surface stuff.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:43.740
I'm sure there are other things that I can't think of and they didn't think of in this game.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:50.500
But you also don't want to go too far with that, and then it starts to feel gimmicky.

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:54.200
But, yeah, I don't think hazards was the way to go.

00:23:55.260 --> 00:24:01.520
Keep in mind, Mario Tennis on the Nintendo 64, you know, one of the all-time great sports games,

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:06.340
did choose a balance of, like, being like, yeah, okay, you can play regular tennis whenever you want,

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:11.880
but then they also made these fun stages that were more, like – they weren't part of the championship modes or anything,

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:15.440
but just something where now there's hazards that affect everybody, you know,

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:19.500
kind of like how Super Smash Brothers has now at least decided to, like –

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.480
you could turn on or off the hazards based on how you want to play.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:23.820
You want to be competitive? Do this.

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:29.860
But if you also just want to, like, embrace the wackiness of where you're playing and the stages and stuff like that,

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:34.620
you know, there's Mount Fuji's in the background of one of the Japanese stage,

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:39.540
and, like you were saying about Kenya, you know, I'm not sure what they could have done anyway with these things.

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:41.640
The design choices alone are nice, right?

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:46.260
The Statue of Liberty for the United States, like, it's nice just to have something.

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:48.540
Yeah, the music and the backgrounds are all cool.

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:51.240
That adds to the variety of it.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:55.460
But, yeah, I also don't know what else they could have done,

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:58.140
but I'm sure someone more creative than us could think of something.

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:00.320
Hmm. We're pretty creative.

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:01.620
We came up with a bunch just now.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:07.040
Yeah. Maybe they could have just done, like, you know, hey, you're in our hometown now,

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:10.680
you play by our house rules, and there's just slight variations on the rules, right?

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:12.380
Yeah. Like, that could be interesting.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:16.100
Like, okay, this rule now is, you know, you don't get the sideline people for this match.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:20.020
Nobody does. Or, you know, everyone gets an extra player for this match or whatever.

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:23.480
Yeah, because to be honest, I don't think the game's taking itself that seriously.

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:27.240
Like, I know we're describing the rules very matter-of-factly and, like, saying, like,

00:25:27.260 --> 00:25:30.280
hey, if you want to play with a friend, this could be a competitive game.

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:39.060
But I don't think the game actually set out to make, like, a new sport or to be so strict about how these things are, you know?

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.660
Well, the super moves sort of speak to that themselves.

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:57.840
Right, right. There is one thing in terms of gameplay that we haven't talked about yet, and, you know, I think we've all said in our versions of dodgeball that we play it in our own neighborhoods,

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:06.880
when you catch the ball, you know, the other player might be out or you might be able to bring someone back in as a result of your catch.

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:09.160
I guess it depends on, like, what kind of rules you're playing with.

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:18.260
In this version of dodgeball, catching the ball doesn't cause a penalty for either side, but it is something that just happens a lot.

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:29.120
Like, I thought catching the ball was a rather easy thing to both time, and if it wasn't a power throw, it was almost, like, inevitable to catch these balls.

00:26:29.120 --> 00:26:35.960
And this is where gameplay really, like, slowed down for me personally, where not that the computers were catching it a lot.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:37.560
They weren't on the easier difficulties.

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:44.740
But I was just always ready, just at the speed that the balls are thrown, other than power throws, ready to catch these balls.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:50.600
And they weren't really taking much damage away from me, and it was just taking me a lot of time to whittle down their damage.

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:59.680
Yeah, I think that, I think it could have helped with, like, making the window a little bit smaller.

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:08.140
Because I think, like, once you hit the catch button, your arms stay open for, like, a second or however many milliseconds.

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:12.940
And if it hits you in that time, then you catch it.

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:19.780
So I guess maybe if they made that a little stricter, it could have felt a little bit more challenging.

00:27:19.780 --> 00:27:23.360
But I also think that you sort of need that.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:26.500
Otherwise, this game is pretty simple.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:32.360
If every ball that's thrown ends up doing damage, I think it would be a bit more boring.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:37.920
Because a lot of what you're doing in this game is trying to figure out, like,

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:44.560
how am I going to sort of, I don't know, like, juke them out and make sure that they take damage.

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:50.320
And that's what makes you need to do the power moves in some instances.

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:55.040
Not always, but I would say it'll definitely help you out.

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:57.160
So, yeah, I agree.

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:06.040
I noticed, definitely noticed that the catching window was way too big of a window.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:10.980
So, both for my side and their side, I thought that they caught the ball a lot, even on, like, normal difficulty.

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:14.900
But, yeah, I do think it needs something like that.

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:18.780
Something to make that more complicated so you're not just hitting every throw.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:21.460
But I feel like it would have been interesting if they...

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:25.520
Right now it's almost, like, easier and more intuitive.

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:29.880
Maybe it's just the way I played to catch the ball rather than to dodge the ball.

00:28:30.020 --> 00:28:31.040
I mean, there is a dodge button.

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:32.120
I don't know if we mentioned it yet.

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:38.280
And I feel like in real dodgeball, catching the ball, the reason you get a reward is because it's a little harder than dodging the ball, right?

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:47.600
So, like, I feel like maybe making dodging the more intuitive primary form of getting away.

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:53.460
And then catching the ball, I know that there's no, right now in this game, there's no penalty or reward for doing it.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:29:00.660
But it feels like maybe there should be because, like, in real dodgeball, catching the ball presents more of a risk, right?

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:01.540
Because you might...

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:05.080
If you don't catch it, you're hit because you haven't dodged.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:15.480
But, so, like, if they could maybe add, like, that to balance it out, like, give you an incentive to try and catch the ball but make it harder and make it more likely you're going to fail and get hit.

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:18.040
But, I don't know that...

00:29:18.040 --> 00:29:31.440
The incentive... I think the incentive is already there, but I still agree that it should be harder because if you just try to dodge the ball, then it's going to make it to their backcourt players and they're going to get...

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:34.020
They're going to keep possession in one way or the other.

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:38.500
But, yeah, you have to balance that out with making catching harder.

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:51.940
Yeah, I think that's overall, like, my biggest complaint with just the game in general with my time with it was that the matches, not that they were terribly long or anything, but that they felt like they could have had a little more energy and speed to them.

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:58.860
Even just with the ball, like, the ball isn't necessarily floaty enough where it actually feels like it's up for grabs.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:05.620
There are very few moments where, like, it does come up to the line and you're able to steal it back, and that's a very exciting thing.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:16.420
I would have liked to see more of that where, like, you can potentially hit a player so hard that the ball might come back to you if another one of your players doesn't, like, rush to grab the ball.

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:28.620
Like you were talking about, Sean, one of the great things that the game did design is that if you duck, there is a good chance it's going to go to the players from your own team that are on the opposite side.

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:32.440
And that's a good thing because that penalizes ducking.

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:37.580
But otherwise, there's really not much opportunity for them to be on that other side.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:42.000
I would have liked to have seen more potential steals and stuff like that because it is in there.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.380
There are ways to intercept the ball.

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:45.980
There's a way to block passes.

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:48.200
It just didn't happen all that frequently.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:50.680
Yeah, the mechanics are there.

00:30:50.680 --> 00:30:55.820
It's just the way the game flows doesn't allow that event to occur too often.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:31:02.820
One other thing I did like, though, is when you're running away from the ball, it does not let you immediately turn around.

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:05.780
And it definitely doesn't let you, like, then catch it.

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:15.500
So if you are running away from this ball, you know, realistically, you know, getting hit from behind hurts more, too, because you're not anticipating it.

00:31:15.500 --> 00:31:17.900
So it also does more damage.

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:20.880
I like those kinds of decisions.

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:30.920
And it does soften the blow of the catch being such a wide open window because there are moments where whether you're prepared to catch it or not, the game won't let you.

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:39.080
Yeah, that would open a lot of opportunities to just cheese this game more so, even though it's not really that difficult of a game.

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:42.280
So that would suck.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:51.340
Yeah. Can we talk about the difficulty a little bit, like the overall difficulty?

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:53.100
Because I'm curious as to what you guys thought.

00:31:53.260 --> 00:32:02.240
But I did think that, at least on normal, after a while, I did get a little, like, fatigued of it just because I felt like I had found a good pattern.

00:32:02.620 --> 00:32:05.440
And I'm just going to stick to it, and it's not going to fail me, you know?

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:07.100
I don't know if you guys felt the same way.

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:14.340
Even on hard mode, I felt like it was definitely harder, but if I just kept sticking to that pattern, it just took way longer for the matches to end.

00:32:15.460 --> 00:32:20.000
I don't know. I felt like there could have been a little more challenge once you really got the hang of it.

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:22.520
Yeah, I felt similarly.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:28.460
I also have played a version of this game before.

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:38.800
I guess I'm skipping a little bit to, like, the sequels and spinoffs, but there was a Game Boy Advance remake, and it was the first game I ever owned on Game Boy Advance.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:41.020
I didn't know that it was based on an NES game then.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:44.460
And it pretty much plays exactly the same.

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:53.920
So I already kind of had some latent strategy, like, deep in the recesses of my Game Boy Advance brain.

00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:58.280
So it was pretty easy from the get-go for me.

00:32:58.420 --> 00:33:01.220
But for me, that didn't make it less fun.

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:05.600
But there was less challenge.

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:10.020
So in terms of that, it was a little bit less engaging than if I came in fresh.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:19.460
You know, Joe, I think that's a good point to bring up the difficulty because, you know, the courts change and the teams, you know, you face country to country.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:26.580
And, like, the stats maybe are, like, chosen to be, like, the later countries in the World Cup mode have overall better stats or something.

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:28.280
I can't confirm or deny that.

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:33.180
But in terms of feeling the progression, that is strictly just tied to the difficulty.

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:44.600
And since the overall teams don't necessarily feel that different other than their stats, once you figure, once you, like, move into the next, like, let's say you start easy, right?

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:46.200
And you realize this is a cakewalk.

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:47.460
I need to move it up to normal.

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:50.720
Normal might be hard for, like, a, you know, a little bit of time.

00:33:50.720 --> 00:34:06.160
And then once you figure out, like, you were talking about cheesing it or just being, you know, being open to slowing down the flow of the game and waiting to catch those balls and then doing the same, like, running throws to do a little more damage than your usual throws.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:09.640
Once you, like, learn that stuff, then it's time to move on to the next one.

00:34:09.640 --> 00:34:21.780
You're not going to actually feel, in my opinion, any terms of actual difficulty progression throughout the mode itself just between the three difficulties that you have to choose from.

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:23.640
That's the only part of progression I felt.

00:34:23.860 --> 00:34:33.740
Yeah. I mean, I guess what I really wish would be the case would, you know, there's so much nuance in the team layouts, right?

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:39.680
Like, each, you know, playing against India felt very different than playing against the Soviet Union.

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:45.640
Like, because they just had different layouts of statistics on their different players.

00:34:46.460 --> 00:34:54.180
And I wish that that, that somehow in the game design, like, that was enough to make me have to rethink my strategy for this.

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:57.660
Oh, how do I do it for this, like, really heavy attacking team?

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:00.400
And how do I do it for this, like, really fast defensive team?

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:07.280
But really, it was just like, and maybe I could put more thought into it, but for me, really, it was like, well, I'm doing the same thing.

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:12.680
It looks like it's affecting them slightly differently, but what I've been doing the whole time still seems like my best bet.

00:35:12.760 --> 00:35:14.960
So I'm not going to change the way I'm playing, right?

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:24.000
Yeah, right. For the veteran who's played the game, like, you know, since 1989 and is still playing it today, who knows all of the various teams' stats,

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:29.940
he can then go in and select his lineup based on each team and only make the game even easier.

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:34.700
Yeah. For him, whereas, like, a casual player can still have a fighting chance against these teams.

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:39.960
It just might take more time or, you know, more learning to actually achieve that.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:49.180
Yeah, there's like, and I think Team Russia has, like, the most obvious, like, defining trait,

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:52.260
because I think they have the most health among them.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:36:01.700
Um, and then there's another team that I realized was intercepting the ball more often when I was utilizing, like, the backcourt players more.

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:09.940
Um, but other than that, like, I could pretty much use the same, uh, I could use the same strategy for all of them.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:16.820
Um, and I also think that that would be, that would be ideal if it actually meant that you had to retool, um,

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:22.380
and that it wasn't just like, oh, I could make this even easier or, uh, and more so, like,

00:36:22.460 --> 00:36:24.980
maybe I could make this less hard if I changed my strategy.

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:37.120
And, you know, there's a whole nother mode that we have to talk about, which is the beanball mode.

00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:39.740
Uh, forget all the rules we were talking about.

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:44.720
This one is, uh, the recess version of, like, it's much more freestyle.

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:46.000
There are no teams.

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:47.420
Hit anyone you like.

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:48.760
Everyone's after you.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:53.980
It's a last man standing dodgeball match in an open space where there is no court.

00:36:54.520 --> 00:37:03.340
